sum=prefix[j+1]−prefix[i]

To maximize the sum for j, we need the minimum prefix in range [j-b, j-a].

Use a deque to maintain the minimum prefix efficiently.
---
import java.util.Deque;
import java.util.LinkedList;

class Solution {
    public int maxSubarrSum(int[] arr, int a, int b) {
        int n = arr.length;
        long[] prefix = new long[n + 1];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            prefix[i + 1] = prefix[i] + arr[i];
        }

        long maxSum = Long.MIN_VALUE;
        Deque<Integer> dq = new LinkedList<>();

        for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
            // remove indices out of window [j-b, j-a]
            while (!dq.isEmpty() && dq.peekFirst() < j - b) {
                dq.pollFirst();
            }

            // add prefix[j - a] to deque maintaining increasing order
            if (j - a >= 0) {
                int idx = j - a;
                while (!dq.isEmpty() && prefix[dq.peekLast()] >= prefix[idx]) {
                    dq.pollLast();
                }
                dq.addLast(idx);
            }

            if (!dq.isEmpty()) {
                maxSum = Math.max(maxSum, prefix[j] - prefix[dq.peekFirst()]);
            }
        }

        return (int)maxSum;
    }
}

---

Explanation

prefix[j] - prefix[i] gives sum of subarray arr[i..j-1].

For each j, valid i are in [j-b, j-a].

We maintain a deque to get the minimum prefix in the valid range efficiently.

Time complexity: O(n), Space complexity: O(n).

Example
int[] arr = {4, 5, -1, -2, 6};
int a = 2, b = 4;
Solution sol = new Solution();
System.out.println(sol.maxSubarrSum(arr, a, b)); // Output: 9
